OrionLayer open fix 119 — the multiplayer bar's lights on the wire
===================================================================

Work order 403 (package B). orion2re on the fork's branch `orionlayer`
(v2.0.0), on top of open fix 118 and its README row. Not on
`orionlayer-local`. In src/ext/ext_api.cpp only, under ORION2RE_EXT.

In a hotseat or network game the main screen draws a coloured light for
each player who has pressed TURN (MAINSCR::Draw_Player_Done_Lights,
mainscr.cpp:2958-3005, called from mainscr_main.cpp:1060-1062: x = 0x49 +
n * w, y 5; the lights are dimmed, frame 5, until every human but the
player is done) and, in a network game, a light per diplomacy request
(Draw_Diplomacy_Request_Lights, mainscr.cpp:1067-1100, called at
mainscr_main.cpp:1057-1059: x = 0x1FA - n * w). The numbers are
NETMOX::net.players_hit_next_turn[], MULTPLAY::Hotseat_Player_Status
(multplay.cpp:826-834: 1 is "done"), NETMOX::Humans_Requesting_Diplomacy()
(netmox.cpp:410, which returns net.players_active_mask) and
HAROLD::Get_N_Remaining_Humans(). The wire carried none of them on the main
screen: MPLY belongs to the multiplayer screens' steps.

The change. SerializeState writes "PLRL" before FILM, on every screen, in
a game of type 1, 2 or 3 (nothing in a single-player game): uint8 version
(1), uint8 MOX::game_type, uint8 the lights are bright (n_humans - 1 <= the
count of done players, the test of mainscr.cpp:2993-2995), uint8 the
diplomacy request mask (0 outside a network game, where the original draws
no such lights), uint8 n players (MOX::NUM_PLAYERS), then per player uint8
done (the rule that decides whether the original draws a light for the
player) and uint8 the player's colour. Read only; the functions it calls
only read.

Live: UNVERIFIED — no live run (work order 403 does not run the engine, and
no multiplayer game can be started offline). Built on the scratch clone
`~/claude/clones/403_orion2re` (`linux-debug`, ORION2RE_EXT on); a misspelt
call (`Humans_Requesting_DiplomacyX`) is refused by the compiler.

